{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:2d9df8f299303c601bc0c5ab627874a9858d2808379c708b2dc5be526f024887","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.3},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1: 22 / 31 / 13. I am at 30, and the whole deviation rests on one arithmetic error that two of them made in opposite directions.\n\nCONJUNCT A (ICE BofA MOVE averaging >=105 over 2027-2036): MARGINAL 0.40, against their 0.33 / 0.45 / 0.26. P2 asserted A \"requires seven years at ~112, i.e. a sustained high-vol regime, not spikes,\" with the arithmetic (3 x 90 + 7 x 112)/10 = 105.4. That is scope insensitivity about how a 120-month mean responds to a fat-tailed series. The same bar clears on ONE crisis: (3 yr at 90 + 1.5 yr at 175 + 5.5 yr at 100)/10 = 108. MOVE printed 180+ for most of Q2-Q3 2022 on a single rate shock, and 2022-23 calendar averages were 120-140. So A does not require a sustained regime; it requires (i) a structural baseline near 100 rather than the 2010s' ~68, which 2A's debt arithmetic, convenience-yield erosion, dealer balance sheets that have not scaled with a roughly doubled Treasury float, QE/QT switching and BoJ normalization all support, and my CQ4 path (G7 nominal ~4.3-4.7% in the 2030s, and MOVE is a basis-point not a normalized measure) reinforces; plus (ii) one 12-18 month crisis. P3's counter — the 2026 start at 64-71, the steepest annual drop since 2009 — is the right objection to a high baseline, but he undercut it himself by asserting MOVE \"scales with the yield level\": MOVE at 64-71 with G7 nominals already at 3-4% is evidence AGAINST the level-scaling term he used to build his own reference class, which means the compressed start is about policy certainty, and policy certainty is the most mean-reverting input in the set. 0.40.\n\nCONJUNCT B (>=1 episode of MSCI World -25% peak-to-trough with G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal failing to fall >=50 bp, or rising >=25 bp, during the drawdown window): MARGINAL 0.42. Sub-step 1, P(a -25% MSCI World drawdown somewhere in 2027-2036) = 0.78: the decade base rate is ~0.70-0.80 (5 of 6 decades since 1970 contained one; the 2010s is the miss), and the locked stack raises it — AI-complex names are a large share of index capitalization, AI-Q4 puts 66% on impairment or a two-year revenue-vs-D&A shortfall, and 2A itself models a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock as a live scenario. Sub-step 2, P(bonds fail to hedge | drawdown) = 0.45, between P2's 0.425 and P1's 0.55 and well above P3's 0.38. P3's argument is the best one in Wave 1 and I partly endorse it: the modal trigger is the capex bust, the capex bust is disinflationary, so bonds rally and the conjunct fails — treating AI-Q4's 66% as RAISING CQ5 gets the sign backwards. But he over-corrected on three counts. First, the leg is loose: \"fails to fall by >=50 bps\" is satisfied by a merely FLAT bond market, and a disinflationary equity drawdown that happens to begin from an already-repriced, high-term-premium curve can easily deliver a 30-40 bp rally rather than a 200 bp one — the 2000-02 and 2007-09 rallies he cites started from much higher nominal levels with intact convenience yields. Second, P1's full-sample record is right and is the correction to the 1995-2020 prior: of ~9 drawdowns since 1970, ~5 came with G7 yields flat or rising (1973-74, 1980, 1987, 1990, 2022), and the correlated regime dominates high-inflation, high-term-premium, high-debt eras, which is the environment 2A locks and my CQ2/CQ4 reinforce. Third, the crisis does not have to BE the capex bust: a funding accident, a basis-trade unwind into forced Treasury selling, or an inflation shock can all trigger the drawdown, and each is definitionally a no-rally event. 0.78 x 0.45 = 0.351, lifted to 0.42 for the looseness of the 50 bp leg and for the possibility of a second, differently-caused drawdown in a ten-year window (expected drawdowns ~1.2).\n\nCORRELATION: +0.50. All three got the sign and roughly the magnitude right, and it matters more here than anywhere else in the set: the crisis that prints MOVE at 175 IS the crisis in which bonds stop hedging — fiscal dominance plus correlated repricing plus degraded plumbing is one latent state, observed jointly in 1973-74, 1980-82, 1987 and 2022. I apply almost no conjunction discount, because these are two readings of one variable.\n\nJOINT = 0.40 x 0.42 + 0.50 x sqrt(0.40 x 0.60 x 0.42 x 0.58) = 0.168 + 0.50 x 0.2417 = 0.168 + 0.121 = 0.289 -> 30. Read structurally: this is close to P(a fiscal/rates crisis decade) x P(it manifests in both measures | crisis), i.e. ~0.33 x 0.85, plus a small path where a non-crisis decade still delivers one correlated drawdown.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.165Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month inflation prints at or below 2.2% for three consecutive monthly BEA releases, AND the euro-area HICP core 12-month rate is at or below 2.0% in the same quarter, with neither driven by a measured recession (OECD-38 harmonized unemployment still under 6%).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my central claim that the cold leg is mismeasured and the hot legs persist. My CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.72 to ~0.45 and CQ2 drops from 35 to ~22; CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~18, CQ6-B absorbs the rest (~43). Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 32 to ~60, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre. Does not move CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS and Eurostat published indices for information, professional/technical and financial services show OUTRIGHT annual price declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years, in series that are market-priced rather than cost- or asset-fee-imputed (specifically: BLS PPI for software publishers and for computer systems design, and the HICP COICOP 08.3 / professional-services sub-indices).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies my strongest attack on the Wave-1 panel — that the deflatable slice is only 0.08-0.12 of core and partly counter-cyclically hot. The cold leg is then real at a 0.20-0.25 weight and I lose ~0.30 pp off my core path. CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~17, CQ6-B rises to ~46, CQ2 falls to ~26, and the Wave-1 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis is vindicated against me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO (any vintage) or the OECD Economic Outlook publishes a medium-term China real GDP growth path for 2032-2036 averaging below 2.6%/yr, or China's realized PPP-weighted real growth for calendar 2030 and 2031 both print below 3.0%, with the property-price index still falling year-on-year.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central correction to Wave 1's aging-drag double-count was wrong in the direction that mattered most: the subtraction was not a double-count but a forecast of a China that does keep decelerating. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1 to ~1.75 and P90 from 3.1 to ~2.8; CQ6-A falls from 25 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~17, and P2's 1.6 P50 becomes the better number. Also lowers my CQ4 P50 by ~0.15 through the (r-g) identity.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey (2030 reference year, published ~2032) plus national equivalents show ISIC Q median gross hourly earnings growing more slowly in real terms than ISIC J+K+M over 2026-2030, in a majority of reporting OECD-38 members, with no binding statutory care-pay award enacted in Germany, Japan, the UK or France in that period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the administered-wage-jump mechanism that is the entire reason my CQ1 is 20 rather than 11. CQ1 conjunct B falls from 0.28 to ~0.12 and CQ1 drops from 20 to ~10, i.e. to the Wave-1 centre. Hypothesis plank (a) falls from 7 to ~4. Mildly lowers my OECD core path (care is 0.15 of core at 3.6% in my build), taking CQ2 to ~31 and CQ6-C to ~23.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Strict operational OECD-38 + China data-centre electricity consumption, on the IEA's own reporting basis, is at or below 650 TWh for calendar 2032 (against ~365 TWh in 2024), OR announced hyperscaler capacity is written down by 20%+ AND measured operational TWh falls year-on-year in any year 2029-2033.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my contrarian coupling call that AI-Q3 is TOO LOW (I say P50 ~1,050-1,100 TWh against the unanimous Wave-1 shade-down to 820-900) and specifically my claim that an impairment event impairs the accounting, not the kilowatt-hours. The electricity-pass-through leg of my hot-core path weakens, CQ2 falls to ~30 and CQ6-C to ~21; CQ3 P50 falls ~0.1 through the capital-deepening term; CQ6-D rises to ~14. The unanimous Wave-1 read would be the right one and my central coupling finding would be the ensemble's worst input.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 premortem seat, effort 'inflation'."}}